    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/artaxias-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artaxias-i.jpg" alt="Artaxias I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/artaxias-i">Artaxias I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Artaxias_I.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Leu Numismatik, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Armenian Highlands were first ruled as one by the Kingdom of Urartu, which fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> for the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Ararat</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and then Achaemenid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> overran both in the 6th century BCE. The land became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, held for three centuries by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontid-dynasty">Orontid dynasty</a>, who after Alexander's victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Gaugamela</a> in 331 BCE and the brief tenure of the Macedonian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoptolemus">Neoptolemus</a> returned not as satraps but as kings: Orontes III and Mithridates of Lesser Armenia declared themselves independent. Weakened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, the last Orontid king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-iv">Orontes IV</a>, was overthrown in 201/200 BCE by a Seleucid commander, Artaxias (Artashes) I, who is presumed to have been related to the Orontids himself.</p><p>The Seleucid defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Battle of Magnesia</a> in 190 BCE loosened their hold, and in the same year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxias-i">Artaxias I</a> founded a Hellenistic Armenian state while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zariadres">Zariadres</a> ruled the kingdom of Sophene. When his reign began in 189 BCE, Media, Caucasian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and the Seleucids had seized the kingdom's remote regions; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> records how Artaxias campaigned east to reunite Caspiane and Paytakaran, north to defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a> and regain Gugark, west to recover Karin, Ekeghik and Derjan, and south, after many battles with the Seleucids, Tmorik. He seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yervandashat">Yervandashat</a> and in 185 BCE founded a new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> where the Metsamor joined it, by the heights of Khor Virap below Mount Ararat.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/artaxata"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artaxata.jpg" alt="Artaxata" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Ararat_and_Khor_Virap_Monastery_2,_Ararat_Plain,_Armenia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a> was laid out at the junction of trade routes connecting the Greek world with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> prospered on that traffic. Its position in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araks</a> valley on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> soon made it a centre of bustling economic activity and international trade, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>; the bathhouses, markets, workshops and administrative buildings that sprang up in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxias-i">Artashes I</a> show its wealth, and the city had its own treasury and customs. The remains of the huge walls that Artashes built can still be found, and an amphitheatre was added under Artavasdes II (55–34 BCE).</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a> founded Tigranakert in the 1st century BCE as a new capital more central to his expanding empire; it had 120,000 people, many temples and an amphitheatre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> describes Tigranes' marching host with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, donkeys and mules for baggage, its sheep, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and goats for food and its hoards of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, 'like locusts or the dust of the earth', though the numbers Israelite historians gave were probably exaggerated. Horses had been the pride of the Armenian warrior since ancient times, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a> called the cavalry 'remarkable by the beauty of their horses and armor'. The kingdom's traditional fifteen provinces never all existed at once; in reality it comprised nearly 200 districts of varying size and type.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great.jpg" alt="Tigranes the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Tigranes_II_the_Great,_Antioch_mint.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The army reached its peak under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a>. The author of Judith credits him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and 12,000 cavalry, most likely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cataphract">cataphracts</a> of the kind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> used, and his force consisted mainly of 120,000 infantry and 12,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a>, a Parthian feature too; like the Seleucids he relied on foot soldiers for the bulk. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> speaks of 500,000 in all with camp followers. The royal guard, the Azatavrear, drawn from elite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, numbered 6,000 heavily armoured horsemen in the ancient period, with light cavalry of mounted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> beside them; in war the cavalry rose to 10,000 or at least 20,000, and part of it always patrolled the borders under a general, the sparapet.</p><p>Tigranes used that army to make an empire. He reincorporated Sophene, completed Artaxias' reunification with Lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and Corduene, and in 83 BCE entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> at the invitation of a faction in its civil wars, ruling it peacefully for 17 years. He defeated the Hasmonean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and at its zenith his realm stretched over parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and what is now south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, Syria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, one of the most powerful states of the Roman East. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> conquered the empire in 69 BCE, and the defeat of Armenia's ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> at the Battle of the Lycus made the kingdom a Roman client in 66 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mithra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mithra.jpg" alt="Mithra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taq-e_Bostan_-_High-relief_of_Ardeshir_II_investiture.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philippe Chavin, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Artaxiads ruled a Hellenistic kingdom from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a>, and after Tigranes also from Tigranakert. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> notes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> recognised itself a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in Artaxias' time, and at the height of Tigranes' rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> itself and some Arab tribes were his vassals; the kingdom was bordered by Caucasian Albania to the east, Iberia to the north along the Kur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to the west and Parthia to the south. Traditionally Greater Armenia was divided into fifteen provinces, which all existed at some time but never together, while Lesser Armenia, a region of the Kingdom of Pontus, stayed outside. Under Rome the kings ruled as clients, sometimes with Roman partners: Augustus installed Tigranes V in 6 CE, who ruled with Erato of Armenia, and the Artaxiads were overthrown in 12 CE for their possible allegiance to Parthia.</p><p>Religion before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> was a pantheon headed by Aramazd, cognate of the Iranian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a> and identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, with Anahit, goddess of fertility and birth, whose temples stood at Armavir, Artashat and Ashtishat; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mihr</a>, god of the sun and light, at Bagaharich and Garni; Vahagn the storm god and dragon slayer at Derik; Tir, god of wisdom, near Artashat; Nane, goddess of war and motherhood; and Hayk, legendary forefather of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and slayer of the Titan Bel. Until the late Parthian period Armenia was predominantly Zoroastrian, and the founder of the later Arsacid line, Tiridates I, was a Zoroastrian priest or magus.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vagharshapat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vagharshapat.jpg" alt="Vagharshapat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vagharshapat">Vagharshapat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D4%B7%D5%BB%D5%B4%D5%AB%D5%A1%D5%AE%D5%B6%D5%AB_%D5%84%D5%A1%D5%B5%D6%80_%D5%8F%D5%A1%D5%B3%D5%A1%D6%80.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Areg Amirkhanian, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For a brief period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a> raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> into an empire that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and Albania through Cappadocia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, Osroene, Adiabene, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, Commagene and Sophene to Judea and Atropatene. Its collapse before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> made Armenia the hinge of the eastern frontier: a client kingdom de jure, a buffer between Rome and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> after Augustus' truce of 20 BCE, and for centuries the prize over which the two powers set up rival kings. In 66 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> crowned Tiridates I, a Parthian Arsacid, under the Treaty of Rhandeia of 63; contemporary Roman writers thought Nero had in fact yielded Armenia to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>.</p><p>The Artaxiad capital endured. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artashat</a> kept its importance until it lost its status as capital, and the kingdom's cities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vagharshapat">Vagharshapat</a>, later Ejmiatsin, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvin">Dvin</a>, built by Khosrov III in 335, carried Armenian statehood on under the Arsacids. That later line made Armenia, in 301 or 314, the first state in history to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> officially, and gave it the alphabet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesrop-mashtots">Mesrop Mashtots</a> in 405. By the 2nd century BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> says, the people of Greater Armenia spoke Armenian, implying that modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> descend from the population Artaxias ruled. The kingdom founded in 189 BCE lasted, through its two dynasties, until 428, when the nobility overthrew the last king and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> installed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marzban">marzban</a> in his place.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pompey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pompey.jpg" alt="Pompey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Pompey_the_Great,_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Didier Descouens, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire of Tigranes lasted from 83 to 69 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> conquered it in 69 BCE, and the final defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>'s ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> at the Battle of the Lycus made Armenia a Roman client kingdom in 66 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> invaded and defeated the kingdom in 34 BCE, but Rome lost hegemony in the Final War of the Republic, 32–30 BCE, and in 20 BCE Augustus negotiated a truce with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> that left Armenia a buffer zone between the two powers. The remaining Artaxiad kings ruled as clients of Rome: Augustus installed Tigranes V in 6 CE, who ruled with Erato of Armenia, and in 12 CE the dynasty was overthrown because of its possible allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>.</p><p>What followed was half a century of contest. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> installed Mithridates of Armenia, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> arrested and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> restored; the Parthians forced submission in 37 CE, Rome retook the kingdom in 47 CE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhadamistus">Rhadamistus</a>' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a> seized it in 51 CE and Tigranes VI ruled from 58 CE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>'s war of 55–63 against Vologases I of Parthia ended in the Treaty of Rhandeia of 63, by which Parthian Arsacids would rule Armenia as Roman clients, and in 66 CE Nero crowned Tiridates I. The Arsacid kingdom survived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s annexation of 114–118, the Sasanian occupation of 252 and the restoration of Tiridates III in 287, adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and was partitioned between the Eastern Roman and Sasanian empires in 387; in 428 the nobility deposed the last king and a Sasanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marzban">marzban</a> took his place.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Artaxiad Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxias-i">Artaxias I</a> in 189 BCE with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a>, united the Armenian Highlands and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a> (83–69 BCE) ruled an empire from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>; conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 69 BCE and a client kingdom from 66 BCE, its last kings fell in 12 CE.</em></p>

